  <content>
    <section class="mini-lesson-question" id="sect-00001">  <title>Question #1: Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>

      <para id="para-00001">Which expression is correct?</para>
    </section>

  <section id="sect-00002"><title>Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>
<para id="para-00002">In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para id="para-00003">In the expression \(y^4 \cdot y^2\) :</para>
<para id="para-00004">\(y^4=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para id="para-00005">The exponent 4 means there are 4 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para id="para-00006">\(y^2=y \cdot y\)</para>
<para id="para-00007">The exponent 2 means there are 2 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para id="para-00008">\(y^4 \cdot y^2=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\).</para>
<para id="para-00009">There are 6 factors of \(y\), which can be written as \(y^6\).</para>
<para id="para-00010">\(y^4 \cdot y^2=y^6\)</para>
<para id="para-00011">Notice the exponent is the sum of the exponents of the factors.</para>
<para id="para-00012">When you multiply exponential expressions with the same base, you keep the base and add the exponents.</para>
<note id="note-00001">
  <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Product Property for Exponents</emphasis></para>
  
  <para id="para-00014">If \(a\) is a real number and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para id="para-00015">\(a^m \cdot a^n=a^{m+n}\)</para>
  <para id="para-00016">To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
</note>

<para id="para-00017">In the expression \(\frac {y^5} {y^3}\):</para>
<para id="para-00018">\(y^5=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para id="para-00019">The exponent 5 means there are 5 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para id="para-00020">\(y^3=y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para id="para-00021">The exponent 3 means there are 3 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para id="para-00022">
  <m:math>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:msup>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mn>5</m:mn>
      </m:msup>
      <m:msup>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
      </m:msup>
    </m:mfrac>
    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
      </m:mrow>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
      </m:mrow>
    </m:mfrac>
    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
    <m:mo>·</m:mo>
    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
  </m:math>
</para>
<para id="para-00023">There are 2 factors of \(y\), which can be written as \(y^2\).</para>
<para id="para-00024">\(\frac {y^5}{y^3}=y^2\)</para>
<para id="para-00025">Notice the exponent is the difference of the exponents of the factors.</para>
<para id="para-00026">When you divide exponential expressions with the same base, you keep the base and subtract the exponents.</para>

<note id="note-00002">
  <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Quotient Property for Exponents</emphasis></para>
  
  <para id="para-00028">If \(a\) is a real number, \(a\neq0\), and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para id="para-00029">\(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\), \(m>n\) and \(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=\frac1{a^{n-m}}\), \(n>m\)</para>
</note>

<para id="para-00030">Here’s how to simplify expressions using properties of exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
  <item>\(x^2 \cdot x^3\)</item>
</list>
  <para id="para-00031">The bases are the same. To multiply, use the Product Property for Exponents.</para>
  <para id="para-00032">\(x^2 \cdot x^3=x^{2+3}=x^5\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
  <item>\(y \cdot y^4\)</item>
</list>
  <para id="para-00033">The bases are the same. To multiply, use the Product Property for Exponents.</para>
  <para id="para-00034">Remember: A variable with no exponent shown has an exponent of \(1\), \(y=y^1\).</para>
  <para id="para-00035">\(y \cdot y^4=y^1 \cdot y^4=y^{1+4}=y^5\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
  <item>\(\frac {x^5}{x^2}\)</item>
</list>
  <para id="para-00036">The bases are the same. To divide, use the Quotient Property of Exponents.</para>
  <para id="para-00037">\(\frac {x^5}{x^2}=x^{5-2}=x^3\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
  <item>\(\frac {x^4yz}{x^2y^2z}\)</item>
</list>
  <para id="para-00038">The expression contains like bases. Use the Quotient Property of Exponents.</para>
  <para id="para-00039">\(\frac{x^4yz}{x^2y^2z}=\frac{x^4y^1z^1}{x^2y^2z^1}=\frac{x^{4-2}z^{1-1}}{y^{2-1}}=\frac{x^2z^0}{y^1}=\frac{x^2}y\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>
  <para id="para-00040">Remember: Any nonzero number to the zero power is 1.</para>

<note class="try" id="note-00003">
<title>Try It: Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00041">Simplify the expressions.</para>

    

    <para id="para-00042">\(x^3 \cdot x^4\)</para>
    <para id="para-00043">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00044">Here is how to simplify expressions using properties of exponents.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00045">\(x^3 \cdot x^4=x^{3+4}=x^7\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>

</solution></exercise>


<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">

    <para id="para-00046">\(\frac {x^9}{x^3}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00047">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
<para id="para-00048">Compare your answer:</para>

<para id="para-00049">\(\frac {x^9}{x^3}=x^{9-3}=x^6\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>



</solution></exercise>

<!--Q#2-->
<!--Interaction End -->
<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">

    <para id="para-00050">\(\frac {x^4y^2z^5}{x^6yz^3}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00051">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
<para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>

<para id="para-00053">\(\frac {x^4y^2z^5}{x^6yz^3}=\frac {y^{2-1}z^{5-3}}{x^{6-4}}=\frac {yz^2}{x^2}\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>

</solution></exercise>

<!--Q#3-->
<!--Interaction End -->

</note>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003"><title>Check Your Understanding</title>
  <exercise id="d195dacc-9ba7-4549-9592-a1cf623a1447" class="multiple-choice">
    <problem id="prob-00004">
      <para id="para-00054">Which expression is equivalent to \(y^6\)?</para>
      <para id="para-00055">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00056">\(y^2 \cdot y^3\)</para>
      <para id="para-00057">\(y \cdot y^6\)</para>
      <para id="para-00058">\(\frac {y^{12}}{y^2}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00059">\(\frac {y^{12}}{y^6}\)</para>
    </problem>
    <solution id="sol-00004">
      <para id="para-00060">Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in the mini-lesson review.</para>
    </solution></exercise></section>
